        ans: 2,
        exp: "Rule: The critical boundary between larceny by trick and false pretenses turns on whether the victim intended to part with mere possession/custody or full title (ownership). Larceny by trick occurs when the defendant obtains mere possession of another's personal property by means of an intentional false statement or fraud. False pretenses requires obtaining title to the property through an intentional false representation of material fact. In a temporary charter delivery arrangement, the owner intends to convey only temporary possessory custody for transport, retaining full legal title and ownership in himself. Because the yacht owner intended to transfer mere possession rather than legal ownership or title, the fraudulent acquisition constitutes larceny by trick rather than false pretenses (Option C). Option A is incorrect because false pretenses strictly requires the transfer of title. Option B is incorrect because larceny by trick is the specific fraudulent form of common law larceny present here. Option D is incorrect because embezzlement requires that the initial acquisition of possession be lawful and non-fraudulent."
